The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (total_allocated < size) {
    err = -ENOSPC;
    goto err_free;
}
```
Reason:
The `alloc_range()` function was incorrectly returning a success status even when the total allocated size was less than the required size. This oversight meant that the function could indicate successful memory allocation while actually failing to allocate enough memory, leading to display corruption in certain scenarios.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (total_allocated < size) {
    err = -ENOSPC;
    goto err_free;
}
```
Reason:
The patch adds a check to ensure that if the total allocated size is less than the requested size, the function will return an error code `-ENOSPC`. This change ensures that the function accurately reflects the allocation status, preventing the display corruption issue by signaling that the memory allocation was insufficient.

